"A data lineage diagram traces datasets or fields through the transformations that produced downstream information.\n\nUse when: Assess the impact of changing a source field. Explain where a report value originated and how it was derived.\n\nAvoid when: Only task scheduling or service calls are relevant.\n\nVisual grammar: Dataset or field nodes identify information assets at a declared granularity. Transformation nodes or labeled edges state derivation. Run, revision, and evidence annotations distinguish observed lineage from declared lineage.\n\nBuild: Choose dataset or column scope and the relevant revision or run. Collect inputs and outputs from lineage events or verified transformation definitions. Add expressions for nontrivial field derivations. Order sources before outputs and route shared ancestors through clear fan-out corridors.\n\nCheck: Lineage granularity is consistent or explicitly nested. Declared dependencies are not presented as observed execution. Derived fields identify the contributing inputs and transformations. Edge labels do not overlap dataset headers or column rows.\n\nPitfalls: Treating orchestration dependency as proof of data derivation. Claiming complete lineage despite uninstrumented transformation steps.\n\nExample: net_sales derives from order_lines.amount minus refunds.amount through a named aggregation job.\n\nAlternatives: Orchestration DAG, Data-flow diagram."
